# Template for `supabase/.env`, which is untracked because it holds secrets. Copy it:
#
#     cp supabase/.env.example supabase/.env
#
# and then `supabase stop && supabase start` — the local Edge Runtime reads this file once, at
# start, so editing it under a running stack changes nothing.

# The origin the functions will accept as the website. `_shared/http.ts` compares the caller's
# Origin against exactly this string; anything else is answered `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: null`
# and the browser discards the reply.
#
# It is set to the port `smoke:web` serves on rather than the 3100 of `pnpm dev:web`, because the
# harness is the thing that breaks confusingly without it: the website comes up, signs in, renders
# the shortlist, and then spins on "Working…" for every travel time with only a console message to
# say why. Change it to http://127.0.0.1:3100 if you want travel to resolve under `pnpm dev:web`.
WEB_APP_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:3199

# The vision model's key, used only by the `analyse` function. No harness needs it — none of them
# analyse anything — so it can stay empty locally.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# TfL's journey planner, from https://api-portal.tfl.gov.uk, used only by the `travel` function.
# Optional — TfL answers unkeyed — but the allowance is per minute and the tiers are far apart: 50
# requests a minute without a key, 500 with one. The backfill schedule is sized to sit inside the
# keyed allowance alongside people browsing. Empty locally is fine; no harness reaches TfL.
#
# A subscription comes with two keys so one can be rotated while the other keeps serving. They are
# two credentials against one quota, so sending them alternately buys nothing; the primary is used
# and the secondary is the standby. Deployed, they are secrets on the functions and nowhere else —
# a key in the workspace-root `.env` would be read by nothing, and the whole point of resolving
# travel server-side is that it never reaches a bundle:
#   supabase secrets set --project-ref <ref> TFL_PRIMARY_KEY=... TFL_SECONDARY_KEY=...
TFL_PRIMARY_KEY=
TFL_SECONDARY_KEY=

# What the pg_cron backfill schedule sends in `X-Backfill-Token` to prove it is the schedule rather
# than a person. Empty locally is right: no harness runs a backfill, and an unset token is not a
# wildcard — `travel` answers "not the schedule" to every caller and falls through to the ordinary
# person's checks. Deployed, this must be the same string the vault holds as `travel_backfill_token`
# (SETUP.md sets both from one shell so they cannot drift):
#   supabase secrets set --project-ref <ref> TRAVEL_BACKFILL_TOKEN=...
TRAVEL_BACKFILL_TOKEN=
